<body>
    <h1>AI+ Scheduling and Inference Management</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <h3 id=3D"AI+SchedulingandInferenceManagement-Architecture"><strong=
>Architecture</strong></h3>
<p>The proposed design is a decoupled, event-driven system centered around =
a persistent state store and a dedicated inference server. This model separ=
ates application logic from resource-intensive model execution, ensuring re=
liability, scalability, and efficient resource management.</p>
<h4 id=3D"AI+SchedulingandInferenceManagement-SystemDiagram"><strong>System=
 Diagram</strong></h4>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">+------=
-----------+   1. Job Request   +-----------------+
|   User/Client   | -----------------&gt; | Coordinator API |
+-----------------+                    +-------+---------+
                                               |
                                               | 2. Create Job in Redis
                                               |    &amp; Notify Scheduler
                                               v
+--------------------------------+     +-------+---------+     +-----------=
-------------+
| Redis                          | &lt;--4b| Central         | 4a. | Rabbit=
MQ               |
| (Job State).                   | ----&gt;| Scheduler       | &lt;---| (Ev=
ent Trigger)        |
+--------------------------------+      +-------+---------+     +----------=
--------------+
             ^                             |
             | 8. Update Final State       | 5. Dispatch Task
             |                             |
   +---------+-----------------------------+------------------------------+
   |                                                                      |
   v                                                                      v
+-----------------------------+                             +--------------=
----------+
| Pre/Post-processing Worker  |                             |    CPU-Only W=
orker     |
| (e.g., VLM Logic)           |                             | (e.g., Transc=
ription)  |
+-------------+---------------+                             +--------------=
----------+
              |
              | 6. Inference Request
              v
+-----------------------------+       7. Manages GPU        +--------------=
--+
| NVIDIA Triton Server        | --------------------------&gt; |   GPU Hard=
ware |
+-----------------------------+                             +--------------=
--+

</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h4 id=3D"AI+SchedulingandInferenceManagement-ComponentSummary"><strong>Com=
ponent Summary</strong></h4>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Coordinator API:</strong> The public-facing entry point that val=
idates requests and creates initial job records in Redis.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Redis:</strong> The <strong>Single Source of Truth</strong> for =
the entire system, storing all job states and resource locks.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>RabbitMQ:</strong> A lightweight message bus used to send event =
notifications (e.g., "new job created") to trigger the scheduler.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Central Scheduler:</strong> The "brain" of the system. It reads =
state from Redis and dispatches tasks to appropriate workers based on resou=
rce availability.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Workers (Pre/Post-processing &amp; CPU-Only):</strong> Stateless=
 containers that execute the business logic of a task. They read job detail=
s from Redis, perform work (which may include calling Triton), and write re=
sults back to Redis.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>NVIDIA Triton Server:</strong> A dedicated, high-performance ser=
vice that runs the AI models, manages GPU memory, and handles all low-level=
 inference operations.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"AI+SchedulingandInferenceManagement-SchedulerandCoordinatorArchit=
ecture"><strong>Scheduler and Coordinator Architecture</strong></h3>
<p>The Central Scheduler is the operational brain of the entire AI workflow=
. It replaces a simple first-in, first-out queueing model with an intellige=
nt, state-aware dispatch system. Its primary responsibility is to orchestra=
te the execution of all jobs by allocating tasks to the appropriate workers=
 based on job requirements and real-time resource availability.</p>
<h4 id=3D"AI+SchedulingandInferenceManagement-CoreLogicandOperation"><stron=
g>Core Logic and Operation</strong></h4>
<p>The scheduler is fundamentally <strong>state-driven and resource-aware</=
strong>, not queue-driven. It operates on a continuous loop, making decisio=
ns by querying Redis, which serves as the single source of truth.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>State-Driven Operation:</strong> The scheduler does not pull job=
s from a RabbitMQ queue. Instead, it directly queries Redis for all jobs wi=
th a <code>status</code> of <code>pending</code>. This allows it to have a =
complete view of all available work at all times.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>GPU-Aware Dispatch:</strong> Before dispatching any GPU-intensiv=
e job, the scheduler checks for the existence of a distributed lock in Redi=
s (e.g., a key named <code>gpu:0:lock</code>). It will only dispatch a job =
with <code>gpu_requirement: "high"</code> if this lock is not present.</p><=
/li>
<li>
<p><strong>Parallel Execution:</strong> The scheduler's logic is designed f=
or maximum throughput. While the GPU is busy processing a long-running task=
, the scheduler continues its loop, actively looking for and dispatching jo=
bs with <code>gpu_requirement: "none"</code> to CPU-only workers.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Priority Handling:</strong> Because it queries the entire set of=
 pending jobs, the scheduler can easily implement prioritization. It can so=
rt the pending jobs by a <code>priority</code> field before selecting the n=
ext one to run.</p></li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"AI+SchedulingandInferenceManagement-WorkflowandTriggers"><strong>=
Workflow and Triggers</strong></h4>
<p>The scheduler is triggered to run its decision loop through a hybrid mod=
el, making it both responsive and resilient.</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Event-Driven Trigger (RabbitMQ):</strong> When a new job is crea=
ted or a worker releases the GPU lock, it can publish a lightweight, conten=
t-free message to a RabbitMQ queue that the scheduler listens to. This prom=
pts the scheduler to immediately run its loop, ensuring no time is wasted.<=
/p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Periodic Trigger (Timer):</strong> As a fallback and for general=
 resilience, the scheduler also runs its loop on a regular, short interval =
(e.g., every 1-2 seconds). This ensures that any missed messages do not hal=
t the system.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>The core of the scheduler can be implemented as a standalone service run=
ning the following logic:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">def sch=
eduler_loop():
    while True:
        # 1. Check GPU Resource State from Redis
        gpu_is_busy =3D redis_client.exists("gpu:0:lock")

        # 2. Handle GPU-Bound Jobs if the GPU is free
        if not gpu_is_busy:
            # Query Redis for the highest priority pending GPU job
            job_id =3D find_pending_gpu_job_from_redis()
            if job_id:
                # Atomically lock GPU and update job status
                redis_client.set("gpu:0:lock", job_id)
                update_job_status_in_redis(job_id, "running")
               =20
                # Dispatch to the appropriate worker
                dispatch_job_via_http(job_id)

        # 3. Handle CPU-Bound Jobs (always, in parallel)
        cpu_job_id =3D find_pending_cpu_job_from_redis()
        if cpu_job_id:
            update_job_status_in_redis(cpu_job_id, "running")
            dispatch_job_via_http(cpu_job_id)

        # Wait for the next cycle or an event trigger
        wait_for_trigger_or_timeout(2.0)
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h4 id=3D"AI+SchedulingandInferenceManagement-SummaryofResponsibilities"><s=
trong>Summary of Responsibilities</strong></h4>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Continuously monitor the state of pending jobs and resources in Redis.</=
p></li>
<li>
<p>Managing the distributed lock for the GPU resource.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Dispatching jobs to appropriate worker services.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Updating job statuses in Redis from <code>pending</code> to <code>runnin=
g</code>.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Operating independently of the workers, ensuring the system is resilient=
 to worker failures.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"AI+SchedulingandInferenceManagement-JobLifecycleandStateManagemen=
t"><strong>Job Lifecycle and State Management</strong></h3>
<p>Job management is elevated from an implicit, in-app concern to an explic=
it, centralized process. The entire lifecycle of a job, from creation to co=
mpletion, is tracked through a state machine within Redis, which acts as th=
e single source of truth. This approach provides complete visibility, resil=
ience, and a clear record for every task processed by the system.</p>
<h4 id=3D"AI+SchedulingandInferenceManagement-TheJobObject:SingleSourceofTr=
uth"><strong>The Job Object: Single Source of Truth</strong></h4>
<p>Every task submitted to the system is represented by a single hash objec=
t in Redis, keyed by a unique <code>job_id</code>. This object contains all=
 information related to the job and is the authoritative record of its stat=
e.</p>
<p>A standard job object includes the following fields:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><code>job_id</code>: A unique identifier (e.g., UUID) generated by the C=
oordinator upon creation.</p></li>
<li>
<p><code>status</code>: The current state of the job in its lifecycle (e.g.=
, <code>pending</code>, <code>running</code>, <code>completed</code>, <code=
>failed</code>).</p></li>
<li>
<p><code>task_type</code>: A string identifying the type of work to be done=
 (e.g., <code>vlm_processing</code>, <code>audio_transcription</code>).</p>=
</li>
<li>
<p><code>gpu_requirement</code>: Specifies the required hardware resource (=
<code>high</code> or <code>none</code>). This is critical for the Scheduler=
.</p></li>
<li>
<p><code>priority</code>: A numerical priority (e.g., 1-10) to allow the Sc=
heduler to prioritize important jobs.</p></li>
<li>
<p><code>created_at</code>: The UTC timestamp of the job's creation.</p></l=
i>
<li>
<p><code>updated_at</code>: The UTC timestamp of the last status change.</p=
></li>
<li>
<p><code>input_payload</code>: A JSON string containing the inputs for the =
job, such as file paths, user prompts, or other parameters.</p></li>
<li>
<p><code>result</code>: A JSON string containing the output of a successful=
ly completed job.</p></li>
<li>
<p><code>error_message</code>: A string detailing the reason for failure if=
 the job's status is <code>failed</code>.</p></li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"AI+SchedulingandInferenceManagement-JobStateLifecycle"><strong>Jo=
b State Lifecycle</strong></h4>
<p>A job progresses through a well-defined state machine. The <code>status<=
/code> field in the Redis hash is updated at each transition.</p>
<p></p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">       =
               +-----------+
                      | Scheduler |
                      | Dispatches|
                      +-----+-----+
                            |
                            v
[ Pending ] ---------&gt; [ Running ]
                            |
            +---------------+---------------+
            |                               |
      +-----v-----+                  +------v----+
      | Worker    |                  | Worker    |
      | Completes |                  | Fails     |
      +-----------+                  +-----------+
            |                               |
            v                               v
      [ Completed ]                    [ Failed ]
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<ul>
<li>
<p><code>pending</code>: The initial state. The job has been created and sa=
ved to Redis but has not yet been selected for execution by the Scheduler.<=
/p></li>
<li>
<p><code>running</code>: The Scheduler has selected the job for execution a=
nd has dispatched it to a worker. For GPU jobs, this state is set concurren=
tly with the acquisition of the GPU lock.</p></li>
<li>
<p><code>completed</code>: A terminal state. The worker has successfully fi=
nished the task, written the output to the <code>result</code> field, and r=
eleased any acquired resources.</p></li>
<li>
<p><code>failed</code>: A terminal state. The worker encountered an unrecov=
erable error during processing and has written details to the <code>error_m=
essage</code> field.</p></li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"AI+SchedulingandInferenceManagement-ComponentResponsibilities"><s=
trong>Component Responsibilities</strong></h4>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Coordinator:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Creates</strong> the job object in Redis with <code>status: "pen=
ding"</code>.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Populates all initial fields, including <code>job_id</code>, <code>task_=
type</code>, <code>input_payload</code>, and <code>gpu_requirement</code>.<=
/p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Scheduler:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Reads</strong> jobs with <code>status: "pending"</code>.</p></li=
>
<li>
<p>Upon dispatching a job, <strong>updates</strong> its status to <code>run=
ning</code>.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Worker (Pre/Post-processing or CPU-Only):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Receives a <code>job_id</code> from the Scheduler.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Reads</strong> the full job object from Redis to get its instruc=
tions.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Performs the core business logic.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Writes</strong> the output to the <code>result</code> field or e=
rror details to the <code>error_message</code> field.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Updates</strong> the job's status to its terminal state (<code>c=
ompleted</code> or <code>failed</code>).</p></li>
<li>
<p>For GPU jobs, its final action is to <strong>release the GPU lock</stron=
g> in Redis.</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"AI+SchedulingandInferenceManagement-DataPersistenceandRetention">=
<strong>Data Persistence and Retention</strong></h4>
<p>To ensure durability, Redis is configured with a persistence mechanism (=
e.g., AOF - Append Only File) to prevent the loss of active jobs during a r=
estart. To prevent the database from growing indefinitely, a Time-To-Live (=
TTL) is set on all jobs once they enter a terminal state (<code>completed</=
code> or <code>failed</code>), ensuring automatic cleanup of old records af=
ter a configured period (e.g., 24 hours or so).<br><br><br>
Here is the augmented wiki section, incorporating your latest implementatio=
n insights, subtle Triton behaviors, and pain points encountered during the=
 Gemma-3n deployment:</p>
<p><strong>NVIDIA Triton</strong></p>
<p>This section details the move from in-app model management to a dedicate=
d, high-performance inference server. The NVIDIA Triton Inference Server be=
comes the sole component responsible for direct GPU interaction, loading an=
d unloading models, and executing inference requests. This standardizes mod=
el serving and abstracts the hardware complexity away from the rest of the =
application.</p>
<p><strong>The Triton Model Repository</strong></p>
<p>Triton discovers and manages models through a structured filesystem dire=
ctory known as the Model Repository. This repository is the central locatio=
n for all models the server can potentially serve. Each model resides in it=
s own subdirectory, which contains its configuration and versioned files.</=
p>
<p>The directory structure is as follows:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">/path/t=
o/model_repository/
=E2=94=94=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 gemma_3n/                # The name of the mode=
l to be used in API calls
    =E2=94=9C=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 config.pbtxt         # The model's configur=
ation file
    =E2=94=94=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 1/                   # A version subdirecto=
ry for the model files
        =E2=94=94=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 ...              # Model files (e.g., T=
ensorRT engine, ONNX file, or Python script)
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Triton monitors this repository and can load new models, unload unused o=
nes, or update to new versions dynamically based on its configuration and p=
olicies.</p>
<p><strong>Model Configuration (config.pbtxt)</strong></p>
<p>Each model directory must contain a config.pbtxt file. This file is the =
explicit declaration of the model's contract and configuration. It defines:=
</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Backend: Specifies which Triton backend should be used to run the model =
(e.g., tensorrt_llm, python, onnxruntime).</p></li>
<li>
<p>Input/Output Tensors: Defines the name, data type, and shape of each inp=
ut and output tensor. This creates a formal API for the model.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Dynamic Batching: Configures how Triton should group incoming requests t=
o maximize GPU throughput.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Instance Groups: Specifies how many copies of the model should be loaded=
 onto one or more GPUs.</p></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Backend Selection</strong></p>
<p>Choosing the right backend is critical for performance and flexibility. =
For a Transformers-based model like gemma-3n, the primary options are:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>TensorRT-LLM Backend: Provides the highest performance. Requires convert=
ing the Hugging Face model to a TensorRT engine. Ideal for static, latency-=
sensitive production use.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Python Backend: Offers the most flexibility. Allows use of native Python=
 libraries (e.g., transformers) within <code>model.py</code>. Ideal for pro=
totype models or complex pre/post-processing that can't be easily separated=
.</p></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Python Backend Subtleties</strong></p>
<p>While powerful, the Python backend introduces several non-obvious pitfal=
ls:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><code>pb_utils.get_input_tensor_by_name(...).as_numpy()</code> returns r=
aw NumPy arrays, not PyTorch tensors. If working with structured inputs (e.=
g., sequences of images), these often must be unpacked, converted to <code>=
PIL.Image</code>, and repackaged for your pipeline.</p></li>
<li>
<p>The processor's <code>apply_chat_template()</code> accepts a Python list=
 of messages. However, the result is a dictionary, and passing it incorrect=
ly to <code>model.generate(**inputs)</code> will raise errors like:<br><cod=
e>TypeError: ... argument after ** must be a mapping, not Tensor</code></p>=
</li>
<li>
<p><code>.to(device)</code> must be applied <strong>per input</strong>, not=
 on the entire dictionary. You must individually call <code>.to(device, dty=
pe)</code> on each tensor inside <code>inputs</code>.</p></li>
<li>
<p><code>CudaSharedMemoryRegion</code> returned from <code>create_shared_me=
mory_region</code> cannot be directly passed to <code>register_cuda_shared_=
memory</code>. You must first call <code>get_raw_handle()</code> to extract=
 the low-level handle bytes. The naming convention here is misleading.</p><=
/li>
</ul>
<p>Example fix:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">raw_han=
dle =3D cuda_shared_memory.get_raw_handle(shm_region)
grpc_client.register_cuda_shared_memory(name, raw_handle, device_id, byte_s=
ize)
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong>Interaction with Pre/Post-processing Workers</strong></p>
<p>The application worker no longer loads any part of the model onto the GP=
U. It acts purely as a network client to Triton.</p>
<p>The worker's responsibilities are strictly defined:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Pre-processing: Executes all high-level logic, such as <code>create_batc=
h_plan()</code> or <code>check_context_window()</code>. It prepares data (e=
.g., prompt strings, NumPy image arrays) to match the tensor API defined in=
 <code>config.pbtxt</code>.</p></li>
<li>
<p>API Call: Packages the data into an HTTP or gRPC request and sends it to=
 the Triton inference endpoint (e.g., <code>POST /v2/models/gemma_3n/infer<=
/code>).</p></li>
<li>
<p>Post-processing: Receives raw output tensors and applies business logic =
(e.g., <code>_extract_narrative()</code>) to produce structured outputs.</p=
></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Limitations</strong></p>
<p>Triton is a highly optimized inference engine. It excels at managing CUD=
A streams, dynamic batching, and concurrent execution. However:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>It has no awareness of, nor does it care about, higher-level business co=
ncepts like job queues or GPU contention across models.</p></li>
<li>
<p>As discussed above, it will not prevent multiple large models from loadi=
ng simultaneously, even if that causes an out-of-memory (OOM) condition. Ou=
r architecture resolves this through our resource management pipeline.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Owns the high-level job queue and prioritization logic</p></li>
<li>
<p>Enforces which models may be loaded at a given time</p></li>
<li>
<p>Prevents GPU contention through controlled model dispatching</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Troubleshooting Insights</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Image batch inputs often require <code>np.ascontiguousarray()</code> and=
 reshaping to avoid type or shape mismatches.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Dynamic batching does not help when using shared memory regions unless <=
code>max_batch_size</code> is set explicitly and batching is enabled in <co=
de>config.pbtxt</code>.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Avoid unnecessary use of <code>Image.resize((224, 224), 224)</code> whic=
h causes <code>Unknown resampling filter</code> errors. Always use the <cod=
e>Image.Resampling.X</code> enum.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Torch will emit a <code>TensorFloat32 tensor cores ... not enabled</code=
> warning. You may resolve this with:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">torch.s=
et_float32_matmul_precision('high')
</pre>
</div>
</div></li>
<li>
<p>The Python backend cannot reuse shared memory regions without proper raw=
 handle rebinding =E2=80=94 even if it previously registered them. Shared m=
emory lifecycle must be tightly controlled.</p></li>
</ul>
<hr>
<p>This separation of concerns between Triton and the Central Scheduler all=
ows each system to play to its strengths, resulting in a stable, GPU-effici=
ent, and scalable architecture. Triton provides performance and flexibility=
. The rest of the system ensures intelligent orchestration.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI+SchedulingandInferenceManagement-Kubernetes"><strong>Kubernete=
s</strong></h3>
<p>In a multi-GPU environment that spans multiple machines, the architectur=
e must evolve to manage these distributed resources. Kubernetes is the only=
 practical way to achieve this, as it provides the necessary primitives for=
 discovery, scheduling, and lifecycle management across machine boundaries.=
</p>
<h4 id=3D"AI+SchedulingandInferenceManagement-ArchitectureforDistributedMan=
agement"><strong>Architecture for Distributed Management</strong></h4>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Granular Resource Tracking (Redis):</strong> The single <code>gp=
u:lock</code> is replaced by a set of granular locks for each GPU on each n=
ode (e.g., <code>gpu:node-a:0:lock</code>, <code>gpu:node-b:0:lock</code>).=
</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Resource Discovery:</strong> A custom Kubernetes Operator is int=
roduced. This operator watches the Kubernetes API. When a new GPU-enabled n=
ode joins the cluster, the operator automatically registers its available G=
PUs in the Redis inventory, making them visible to the scheduler.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Targeted Dispatch (Scheduler):</strong> The scheduler's logic is=
 enhanced:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>It queries Redis for any available GPU lock across the entire cluster.</=
p></li>
<li>
<p>Upon finding and acquiring a specific lock (e.g., <code>gpu:node-b:0:loc=
k</code>), it must ensure the worker pod runs on that specific node.</p></l=
i>
<li>
<p>It achieves this by creating the worker pod with a <code>nodeAffinity</c=
ode> or <code>nodeSelector</code> rule in its manifest, targeting the label=
s of the specific node (<code>node-b</code>).</p></li>
</ol></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Local Triton Interaction:</strong> The worker pod starts on the =
correct node. It then connects to the local Triton instance on that same no=
de and performs the explicit <code>load -&gt; infer -&gt; unload</code> lif=
ecycle. When complete, it releases the specific Redis lock (<code>gpu:node-=
b:0:lock</code>), making that individual GPU available cluster-wide.</p></l=
i>
</ul>
<p></p>
<p></p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
